  "body": "Three storage-only changes to the built-in fonts. The values the renderer sees are\r\nbyte-identical in every case, so nothing repaginates and no section cache invalidates.\r\n\r\n**Measured on this branch vs `develop` (`66a7ae10`), `-e default`:**\r\n\r\n| | bytes | % of app partition |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `develop` | 5,707,408 | 87.1 % |\r\n| this PR | 5,384,080 | **82.2 %** |\r\n| **saved** | **323,328** | **4.9 points** |\r\n\r\n(87.1 % matches the figure quoted in #3083, so the baseline lines up.)\r\n\r\nWhere it comes from:\r\n\r\n| | before | after | delta |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| glyph bitmaps (Zopfli) | 1,359,791 | 1,292,262 | −67,529 |\r\n| kern matrices → sparse | 339,932 | 83,364 | −256,568 |\r\n| kern class maps (split) | 107,661 | 107,661 | ±0 |\r\n\r\n## 1. Sparse (CSR) kern matrix — −256 KB\r\n\r\n**86.6 %** of the dense `leftClass × rightClass` matrix is zero. Storing only the\r\nnon-zero entries — row offsets plus `(column, value)` pairs — is about a quarter\r\nof the size.\r\n\r\n## 2. Split kern class maps — free\r\n\r\nBuilt-in fonts carry parallel `uint16` codepoint / `uint8` classId arrays instead\r\nof packed 3-byte `EpdKernClassEntry` records. Same 3 bytes per entry, but the\r\nbinary search only ever reads codepoints: keeping the payload out of the searched\r\narray shrinks its footprint by a third, and a `uint16` array is naturally aligned\r\nwhere the packed struct leaves two of every three codepoint reads unaligned.\r\n\r\nThis one is here for speed, not size — see below.\r\n\r\n## 3. Zopfli DEFLATE backend — −67 KB\r\n\r\n`--zopfli`, enabled in `convert-builtin-fonts.sh`. Zopfli emits **ordinary\r\nDEFLATE**, so the on-device uzlib inflater decodes it unchanged and at the same\r\nspeed — it is only slower to *compress*, which is free at font-generation time.\r\n\r\n## SD-card fonts are untouched\r\n\r\nThe `.cpfont` format stores the packed class entries and the dense matrix verbatim\r\nand maps them in place (see the `static_assert`s at the top of `SdCardFont.cpp`), so\r\nchanging them would break font files already on users' cards. SD fonts keep both\r\nold forms; `getKerning()` picks the representation by which pointer is non-null, and\r\n`applyKernLigaturePointers()` nulls the new ones explicitly rather than relying on\r\nthe caller's initialisation.\r\n\r\n## Performance\r\n\r\nHost-measured against real font tables driving the real `EpdFont::getKerning`,\r\nmin of 9 runs, sparse+split together vs the original dense+packed:\r\n\r\n| mix | delta |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| realistic text | +2.8 – 4.2 % |\r\n| every pair classed | +3.8 – 5.0 % |\r\n\r\nTwo mixes because most adjacent pairs in real text have no kern class at all and\r\nbail before reaching the matrix.\r\n\r\nIt is small because the row access is not where the time goes: **the two\r\n`lookupKernClass` binary searches are ~96 % of `getKerning`** (59.4 ns of 61.7 ns).\r\nThe sparse matrix alone cost +6.2–6.9 % / +11.0–12.9 %; splitting the class maps\r\npaid most of that back, which is why change 2 is in here.\r\n\r\nThe CSR row is scanned rather than binary-searched — rows hold ~15 entries on\r\naverage, short enough that the scan measured faster (+11 % vs +19 % for\r\n`std::lower_bound` on the worst-case mix).\r\n\r\n## Relationship to #3083\r\n\r\nComplementary rather than competing. #3083 attacks the glyph bitmaps with a new\r\ncodec; this leaves the bitmap format alone (Zopfli is still plain DEFLATE) and takes\r\nthe kerning tables, which are 24 % of font flash and hadn't been looked at. No new\r\nformat, no trained model, no decode-side change — the only runtime cost is the few\r\npercent on `getKerning` above.\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n\r\n- **143/143** host tests pass. Five new ones: sparse-matches-dense,\r\n  split-matches-packed, missing entries, empty rows, and the combination the\r\n  built-in fonts actually ship (split maps **and** sparse matrix).\r\n- Verified non-vacuous by mutation: indexing the CSR values row-relative, and\r\n  dropping the key confirmation from the split lookup, each fail a distinct test.\r\n- `verify_compression.py`: 32 passed, 0 failed, 5 skipped (uncompressed).\r\n- Font regeneration is the plain output of `convert-builtin-fonts.sh`.\r\n\r\n## Possible follow-up, not in this PR\r\n\r\n`GROUP_MAX_UNCOMPRESSED_BYTES` is 65536 and never binds: the largest group on\r\n`develop` is **55,295 B** (`notoserif_18_bolditalic`) and **76 groups exceed 16 KB**.\r\n`FontDecompressor` mallocs a whole group to reach a single glyph, so on a fragmented\r\nheap large-script text (Cyrillic in particular) can lose glyphs. Lowering the cap\r\nfixes it and costs flash, which this PR would fund. Happy to open that separately\r\nwith numbers if there's interest.\r\n",
